    WebSocket-over-HTTP/2 (RFC 8441) slow-handler regression test.

    WebSocketStreamProcessor::notifyIo() used to block on the connection's
    process_mutex, so every stream-data notification that arrived while user code was
    running in gotMessage() parked an async I/O callback worker.  A peer that keeps
    sending during a slow handler parks the whole shared dispatcher pool that way,
    stalling callbacks for every other connection in the process - and deadlocking
    outright when the running handler performs a dispatcher drain (an ordinary
    RestClientIo destructor reaches AsyncIoController::flushCallbacksByOwner()).

    notifyIo() now admits one thread to the frame-drain loop and records further
    notifications in a pending flag instead of blocking on them.  This test pins both
    halves of that contract: the pool stays usable while a handler is busy, and the
    skipped notifications are lossless - every frame is still delivered, in order.

    The HTTP/1.1 async path (HttpWebSocketPollOperation) had the same defect in two places:
    the handler-pool drainer held the connection's dispatch mutex while gotMessage() ran,
    so every frame notification that arrived meanwhile parked a callback worker in
    onPollComplete(), and onComplete() waited on the callback worker for a running
    handler when the peer disconnected.  With the pool parked, no accept completion could
    run, so every listener in the process stopped accepting new connections until the
    handler returned.  The HTTP/1.1 cases pin that a fresh connection is still served
    while handlers are busy, that no frame is lost or reordered, and that a connection
    whose peer disconnects during a busy handler is finished once the handler returns.
*/

%modern

%prepend-module-path "${SCRIPT_DIR}/../../../../qlib"
%requires Util
%requires QUnit
%requires Mime
%requires Logger
%requires HttpServerUtil
%requires HttpServerAsyncIo
%requires HttpServer
%requires WebSocketUtil
%requires WebSocketHandler
%requires WebSocketClient
%requires AsyncSocketIo

%exec-class WebSocketSlowHandlerTest

#! Released by the test to let the first message's handler return
our *Counter ws_hold;

#! Decremented when the first message enters gotMessage()
our *Counter ws_holding;

#! Decremented once per message handled
our *Counter ws_handled;

#! Serializes access to @ref ws_messages
our *Mutex ws_recv_lock;

#! Every message in the order gotMessage() saw it
our *list<string> ws_messages;

#! Decremented once per connection closed on the server side
our *Counter ws_closed;

#! Holds the first message's dispatch until the test releases it
class SlowWsConnection inherits WebSocketConnection {
    private {
        bool held = False;
    }

    constructor(WebSocketHandler handler) : WebSocketConnection(handler) {
    }

    gotMessage(string msg) {
        {
            AutoLock al(ws_recv_lock);
            ws_messages += msg;
        }
        if (!held) {
            held = True;
            ws_holding.dec();
            # user code blocks here for as long as the test likes; this is the window
            # in which every further stream-data notification arrives
            ws_hold.waitForZero(120s);
        }
        ws_handled.dec();
    }

    connectionClosed() {
        if (ws_closed) {
            ws_closed.dec();
        }
    }
}

#! Answers the probe request that shows the server still accepts and serves new connections
class ProbeHandler inherits AbstractHttpRequestHandler {
    hash<HttpResponseInfo> handleRequest(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, *data body) {
        return <HttpResponseInfo>{
            "code": 200,
            "body": "ok",
        };
    }
}

class SlowWsHandler inherits WebSocketHandler {
    constructor(*hash<auto> opts) : WebSocketHandler(NOTHING, opts) {
    }

    WebSocketConnection getConnectionImpl(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, string cid, *hash<auto> conn) {
        return new SlowWsConnection(self);
    }
}

class WebSocketSlowHandlerTest inherits QUnit::Test {
    private {
        HttpServer mServer;
        SlowWsHandler mWsHandler;
        Logger mLogger;
        int mPort;
        string mWsUrl;

        #! Callback worker pool size for this process
        /** Small enough that the pre-fix behaviour - one parked worker per notification
            delivered during the slow handler - exhausts it well inside @ref BusyMessages,
            so the probe below fails instead of merely running slowly.  The cap is read
            when the dispatcher is created, so it is set before the server starts.
        */
        const Workers = 4;

        #! Messages sent while the handler is blocked
        const BusyMessages = 20;

        #! Settle window for the server to receive and dispatch the busy messages
        const Settle = 1s;

        #! Upper bound for any wait that a regression turns into a hang
        const HangTimeout = 30s;

